---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

/**
 * A reference page rather than a tool. Tool pages rarely earn links; a table
 * people cite does. Every number here is a published platform target.
 */

interface Target {
  platform: string;
  lufs: string;
  truePeak: string;
  kind: string;
  note: string;
}

const music: Target[] = [
  {
    platform: 'Spotify',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Normalization is on by default. Louder masters are turned down; quieter ones are turned up only in the “Loud” setting.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Apple Music',
    lufs: '-16',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Sound Check is on by default on iOS. Apple’s mastering guidance also asks for -1 dBTP.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'YouTube',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music & video',
    note: 'Turns loud uploads down but never turns quiet ones up, so undershooting costs you real loudness.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Amazon Music',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-2 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Slightly more conservative peak ceiling than most.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Tidal',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Normalization can be switched off by the listener.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Deezer',
    lufs: '-15',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Sits between the streaming consensus and Apple’s target.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'SoundCloud',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Music',
    note: 'Applies normalization on playback for most listeners.',
  },
];

const spoken: Target[] = [
  {
    platform: 'Apple Podcasts',
    lufs: '-16',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Podcast (stereo)',
    note: 'The de facto podcast standard. Use -19 LUFS for mono files.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Spotify Podcasts',
    lufs: '-14',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Podcast',
    note: 'Matches Spotify’s music target rather than the podcast convention.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Google / YouTube Podcasts',
    lufs: '-16',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Podcast',
    note: 'Follows the -16 convention.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Audible / ACX',
    lufs: '-18 to -23 RMS',
    truePeak: '-3 dBTP',
    kind: 'Audiobook',
    note: 'ACX specifies RMS, not LUFS, and requires a noise floor below -60 dBFS. Its check is strict and automated.',
  },
];

const broadcast: Target[] = [
  {
    platform: 'EBU R128 (Europe)',
    lufs: '-23',
    truePeak: '-1 dBTP',
    kind: 'Broadcast',
    note: 'Tolerance ±0.5 LU. The origin of the modern LUFS approach.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'ATSC A/85 (US)',
    lufs: '-24',
    truePeak: '-2 dBTP',
    kind: 'Broadcast',
    note: 'The CALM Act enforces this for US television.',
  },
  {
    platform: 'Netflix',
    lufs: '-27',
    truePeak: '-2 dBTP',
    kind: 'Streaming video',
    note: 'Measured with dialogue gating rather than programme loudness.',
  },
];

const groups = [
  { name: 'Music streaming', rows: music },
  { name: 'Podcasts and spoken word', rows: spoken },
  { name: 'Broadcast and video', rows: broadcast },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Loudness targets for every streaming platform (LUFS)',
    description:
      'The integrated loudness and true peak targets used by Spotify, Apple Music, YouTube, podcasts and broadcast, with what each platform does to files that miss them.',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
    publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'All tools', item: SITE.url },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Loudness targets',
        item: new URL('/loudness-targets', SITE.url).href,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Loudness Targets by Platform: LUFS Reference Table"
  description="Every streaming and broadcast loudness target in one table (Spotify, Apple Music, YouTube, podcasts, EBU R128) with LUFS and true peak values."
  path="/loudness-targets"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom: var(--s10)">
      <h1>Loudness targets by platform</h1>
      <p class="toolhead__sub">
        What every major platform expects, in LUFS and true peak, and what it
        does to your file if you miss.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom: var(--s10)">
      <p>
        Every streaming service normalizes playback loudness. That single fact
        makes the loudness war pointless: if you master a track four decibels
        louder than the target, Spotify turns it down four decibels on the way
        out, and all you have achieved is squashing your own dynamic range for
        nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The number that matters is <strong>integrated LUFS</strong>, loudness
        averaged across the whole programme, weighted for how human hearing
        actually works and gated so that silence between phrases does not drag
        the reading down. It is defined by ITU-R BS.1770, and it is what all of
        these platforms measure.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      groups.map((group) => (
        <section style="margin-bottom: var(--s12)">
          <h2 style="margin-bottom: var(--s4)">{group.name}</h2>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table class="datatable">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Platform</th>
                  <th scope="col">Target</th>
                  <th scope="col">True peak</th>
                  <th scope="col">Type</th>
                  <th scope="col" style="white-space: normal; min-width: 22rem">
                    Notes
                  </th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {group.rows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row" style="font-weight: 600">
                      {row.platform}
                    </th>
                    <td>{row.lufs} LUFS</td>
                    <td>{row.truePeak}</td>
                    <td>{row.kind}</td>
                    <td style="white-space: normal">{row.note}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Which number should you actually aim for?</h2>
      <p>
        If you are releasing music to every service at once, master to
        <strong>-14 LUFS integrated with a -1 dBTP ceiling</strong>. That satisfies
        the majority outright, and Apple Music turning it down a further two
        decibels is inaudible and harmless. Chasing each platform separately is
        not worth the effort for anything other than a dedicated Apple release.
      </p>
      <p>
        For podcasts, use <strong>-16 LUFS for stereo</strong> and
        <strong>-19 LUFS for mono</strong>. The difference exists because a mono
        file played through two speakers measures roughly 3 LU louder than the
        same material in stereo, so mono needs a lower target to sound equally
        loud.
      </p>
      <p>
        For anything going to broadcast, use the number the broadcaster gives
        you and do not improvise. Broadcast delivery is automatically checked
        and rejected.
      </p>

      <h2>True peak is not the same as peak</h2>
      <p>
        A digital file stores samples, but playback reconstructs a continuous
        waveform that passes through them. And that curve can rise higher than
        any individual sample. A file that shows 0.0 dBFS on a normal peak meter
        can genuinely reach +1.5 dB once a converter or an MP3 encoder
        reconstructs it, which clips.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why every target above specifies a true peak ceiling below zero.
        Lossy encoding makes it worse: the encoder discards information and the
        decoded waveform is not the one you measured, so peaks move. A -1 dBTP
        ceiling is the standard insurance, and -2 dBTP is safer if you are
        encoding to a low bitrate.
      </p>

      <h2>Loudness range, and why quiet passages matter</h2>
      <p>
        Loudness range (LRA, measured in LU) describes how much the loudness
        varies across a programme. A heavily compressed pop master might sit
        around 3–5 LU; an orchestral recording can exceed 15 LU; a well-produced
        podcast usually lands between 5 and 8 LU.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no correct value, but there are wrong ones for a context. A
        podcast with an 18 LU range is unlistenable in a car, because the quiet
        parts vanish under road noise while the loud parts make you reach for
        the volume. If that is your situation, compression before normalization
        is the fix, not a louder target.
      </p>

      <h2>Measure and fix</h2>
      <p>
        You can check any file against this table with the
        <a href="/loudness-meter">loudness meter</a>, which reports integrated
        LUFS, loudness range and true peak using the same BS.1770-4 measurement
        described above. The <a href="/audio-normalizer">audio normalizer</a>
        will then move a file to any target on this page while holding the true
        peak below its ceiling. Both run entirely in your browser.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
